at assumption needs to be challenged. The possible stories are that fee or se=
rvice friction precedes the drop in activity, that regular inflows and recurr=
ing payments indicate a primary relationship, or that customers keep the acco=
unt open but move useful activity elsewhere.

We looked at a version of this before. For the November 2020 review, please s=
how what changed and whether the earlier conclusion still holds. Please start=
 with the decision we need to make, then show whether the available evidence =
is strong enough to support it.

Please prepare a control view with the exceptions, owner and reason each item=
 was included. The output must help us decide how Keystone should define a pr=
imary customer and which customers need service recovery rather than marketin=
g.

Data available: monthly account snapshots, status events, limits, product enr=
olments and signatories; transaction-level timestamps, channels, amounts, sta=
tuses and error context; debit-order mandates, collection dates, suspensions =
and cancellations; customer contacts, complaints and suggestions.

Please state the limitation plainly: Use transaction behaviour as a proxy. Do=
 not claim to observe activity held at another bank.

